AB  - <div data-language="eng" data-ev-field="abstract">The construction sector accounts for nearly 40% of global carbon emissions, underscoring an urgent need for more sustainable building materials. In this context, mycelium-based composites (MBCs)—derived from fungal mycelium—have emerged as promising, lower-impact alternatives to carbon-intensive materials like concrete and polystyrene. This review consolidates cutting-edge research on MBCs’ biological, thermal, and mechanical attributes, and critically evaluates their life cycle performance. MBCs can reach a thermal conductivity as low as 0.03 W/mK, inherent fire resistance from chitin and β-glucans, and a Young's modulus of up to 3.66 GPa, while also achieving net-negative global warming potential (GWP) by sequestering more carbon during cultivation than is emitted throughout their life cycle. However, energy-intensive steps (e.g., sterilization and drying), moisture sensitivity, and mechanical variability pose significant hurdles to broader adoption, highlighting the need for innovations such as renewable energy integration, protective coatings, and standardized testing protocols. Although real-world demonstrations already exhibit promising durability and CO₂-sequestering capabilities, higher production costs and lengthy growth cycles currently limit mass-scale implementation. By bridging knowledge gaps and outlining pathways for upscaling—from fabrication methods to policy frameworks—this review provides a comprehensive foundation for harnessing MBCs’ transformative potential in reducing the construction industry's carbon footprint and propelling it toward a more sustainable, circular economy.<br/></div> © 2025
