AB  - This research aims to investigate the mode of mechanistic coupling of alkali silica reaction (ASR) and the cracks induced by external forces on reinforced concrete (RC). The effects of preceding flexural cracks on the following ASR-induced expansion in RC beams are studied by means of analysis and experiments. It is shown that preceding cracks may affect the magnitude of 3D confinement and the alkali transport, which can influence on the 3D expansion. Complex interactions of multi-directional cracks, ASR-expansions and fatigue lives of structural concrete are discussed under moving fatigue loads on RC bridge deck slabs. Analytical studies are conducted in comparison with the literature on multi-scale modeling and it is recognized that the mechanical properties such as tension transfer by ASR-gel injected inside cracks may possibly affect the overall fatigue performance of RC slabs.<br/> © 2018 Taylor & Francis Group, London.
