AB  - <div data-language="eng" data-ev-field="abstract">Co-firing residual lignocellulosic biomass with fossil fuels is often used to reduce greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions, especially in processes like cement production where fuel costs are critical and residual biomass can be obtained at a low cost. Since plants remove CO<inf>2</inf> from the atmosphere, CO<inf>2</inf> emissions from biomass combustion are often assumed to have zero global warming potential ((Formula presented.) = 0) and do not contribute to climate forcing. However, diverting residual biomass to energy use has recently been shown to increase the atmospheric CO<inf>2</inf> load when compared to business-as-usual (BAU) practices, resulting in (Formula presented.) values between 0 and 1. A detailed process model for a natural gas-fired cement plant producing 4200 megagrams of clinker per day was used to calculate the material and energy flows, as well as the lifecycle emissions associated with cement production without and with diverted biomass (supplying 50% of precalciner energy demand) from forestry and landfill sources. Biomass co-firing reduced natural gas demand in the precalciner of the cement plant by 39% relative to the reference scenario (100% natural gas), but the total demands for thermal, electrical, and diesel (transportation) energy increased by at least 14%. Assuming (Formula presented.) values of zero for biomass combustion, cement's lifecycle GHG intensity changed from the reference (natural gas only) plant by −40, −23, and − 89 kg CO<inf>2</inf>/Mg clinker for diverted biomass from slash burning, forest floor and landfill biomass, respectively. However, using the calculated (Formula presented.) values for diverted biomass from these same fuel sources, the lifecycle GHG intensities changes were −37, +20 and +28 kg CO<inf>2</inf>/Mg clinker, respectively. The switch from decreasing to increasing cement plant GHG emissions (i.e., forest floor or landfill feedstocks scenarios) highlights the importance of calculating and using the (Formula presented.) factor when quantifying lifecycle GHG impacts associated with diverting residual biomass to bioenergy use.<br/></div> © 2023 The Authors. GCB Bioenergy published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.
