AB  - Today the entire modern world is suffering from global warming and its consequences due to population boom and their housing demand. For all constructions, the materials used not only consume massive energy and resources during their manufacture and transportation but also deteriorate the environment by releasing huge amounts of dust, solid waste and greenhouse gases (GHGs). This miserable scenario creates an immediate need for a sustainable alternative material for eco-friendly housing. Rammed earth (RE) provides such a potential, where the natural subsoil is being used as the primary constituent (i.e., silt, clay, sand, gravel) with or without a small percentage of stabilizer (like cement). With 8%–12% water content, the mix is finally tamped into the required shape using simple or pneumatic tools. To verify the environmental and structural stability aspects of this construction, a detailed field investigation for about 9 years, material study and model house preparation, client interview and discussion were done. As an output, this paper outlines the contribution of the building sector to global warming and climate change in its entire life cycle. Moreover, it introduces RE as an effective energy solution focusing on its suitability in terms of sustainability and structural stability by considering its production phase, materials used, overall strength, durability, versatility, thermal performance, earthquake resistance, and several environment-friendly good aspects like low embodied and operating energy requirement, low consumption of resources, and minimal release of GHGs.
