AB  - Ultra-high-performance concrete (UHPC) possesses excellent mechanical properties and durability, effectively overcoming the shortcomings of normal concrete, especially susceptibility to brittle failure and inadequate durability. The superior performance of UHPC contributes to the reduction of life-cycle costs of civil infrastructure. However, UHPC is highly dependent on energy-intensive materials and consumes valuable natural resources, for example, cement, quartz sand, and steel fibers. Furthermore, the production of these raw materials and the preparation of UHPC emit large amounts of CO2. At the same time, the mechanical properties and durability of UHPC structures are prone to degradation in harsh, extreme, multifactor coupled environments, restricting further development of UHPC and their structures toward sustainability. Optimization of the mix proportion, utilization of supplementary cementitious materials, recycled aggregates, and solid waste, as well as the development of new cementing systems are effective measures to achieve further sustainable development of UHPC. This chapter first introduces the basic properties of UHPC and its impact on the environment as well as the optimal mix proportion. Then, the preparation of sustainable ultra-high-performance concrete (SUHPC) using solid waste or new cementitious material systems will be introduced. In addition, this chapter presents the incorporation of high-performance fibers and nanomaterials to fabricate SUHPC, which can reduce the amount of steel fibers and lower the cost of UHPC, develop UHPC with good functionality, as well as further realize the sustainable development of UHPC. Finally, the structural applications of SUHPC are introduced, showing that SUHPC has the potential to further improve the safety and extend the service life of structures. Additionally, it promotes the development of structures toward multifunctionality and intelligence.
