AB  - Mechanical and environmental impacts frequently lead to premature cracking of traditional concrete materials and structures, which brings about risks related to reduced service life. Fortunately, cementitious materials have the capability of autogenous self-healing, which helps with the closure of the cracks through chemical processes, such as calcium carbonate precipitation and continued hydration. However, autogenous self-healing capability is much more pronounced in cases where the cracking is not severe and crack widths are limited. In this regard, new generation construction materials such as engineered cementitious composites (ECC) capable of exhibiting microcracking behaviour even under severe loading conditions hold great promise along with a more recent and eco-friendlier version of ECC, which is called engineered geopolymer composites (EGC). This chapter therefore focuses on the autogenous self-healing capabilities of ECC and EGC, covering the principles and properties together with the brief assessments on the test methods used for the evaluation of self-healing concrete and life cycle analysis.
