AB  - Porous concrete is a promising functional material for phosphorus (P) containing wastewater treatment. Conventional porous concrete is fabricated with gravel and cement, showing low P removal efficiency due to low porosity, inferior surface area, and low active components of gravel. Accordingly, a novel porous concrete (C-A) is synthesized by replacing gravel with alkali-activated aggregate (compressive strength of 90.12 MPa and pore surface area of 5.17 m2/g) derived from industrial solid wastes (ISWs). C-A concrete shows a high porosity (47.24 %), exhibiting a high P adsorption capacity (88.93 mg/g). Both chemical precipitation (Ca-P and Al-P precipitations) and physical adsorption contribute to high removal efficiency of P. Life cycle assessment (LCA) studies illustrates the less carbon emissions (by 13.8 %), less ore resource exploitations, and lower raw material costs (by 15.2 %) of C-A, compared to conventional porous concrete. This study provides a sustainable management strategy for recycling ISWs as functional materials for P pollution control.
