AB  - Wood bio-concretes (WBCs) are composed of a cementitious matrix mixed with wood aggregates. Usually, wood shavings are employed as aggregates mixed with ordinary portland cement and other supplementary cementitious materials (SCMs) such as metakaolin, fly ash, and bio-based ashes. WBCs are circular, low-carbon building materials, especially when waste-based local materials are used as SCM or aggregates. The reviewed literature shows that an evaluation of the main challenges for using WBCs includes the logistical aspects of the wood source, the proper specification for different building applications, the development of a standardization process, and inclusion in building regulations. In terms of the life cycle assessment methodology, it is required improvement in adequate impacts' allocation of waste materials; biogenic carbon modeling and avoided impacts quantification when waste materials are used. This chapter presents valuable information for WBC developers and researchers, which can be adapted to the study of other bio-based composite materials.
