AB  - A major stumbling block in achieving a sustainable world is producing sustainable concrete. To achieve this, alterations in conventional concrete are being instigated in the construction industry. The current practices of utilizing by-products from industries as supplementary cementitious material (SCM) in concrete production have been widely increased in the construction industry satisfying physical and mechanical properties. The present study focuses on the quantification of emissions reduced due to the use of fly ash (FA) and ground granulated blast furnace (GGBS) SCMs throughout the life cycle performance of concrete. The objective of the study is to adopt life cycle assessment (LCA) methodology to investigate and assess the environmental impacts of SCMs-based concrete with varying percentage replacement levels over the conventional concrete. The environmental performance of FA- and GGBS-based mixes was evaluated utilizing Revit and Tally software. The study accessed and compared the eutrophication potential (EP), global warming potential (GWP), acidification (AP), and other parameters of the concrete mixes from cradle to grave boundary conditions. The study revealed that among all mixes, GGBS-based concretes are emitting fewer pollutants and emissions compared to FA-based concretes.
