AB  - Sustainability of structures, including hydraulic gates, is an issue that gains growing attention in recent decades. Yet, there is little useful design guidance in this field. This chapter aims to provide such guidance and to assist engineers in performing sustainability assessments (eco-analyses) of hydraulic gate designs. The proposed methods are inspired by the methodology of life cycle analysis (LCA) that investigates the environmental performances of products “from cradle to grave.” As concerns the life cycle of gates, principal environmental issues are sustainable material choice, sustainable operation, and sustainable maintenance. A thorough, unbiased method of sustainable material selection is presented and worked out for the gate of structural steel, stainless steel, aluminum, composite, concrete, and timber. The considered criteria cover energy consumption, pollution to air, pollution to water, and CO2 emission. The analysis of gate operation covers mainly the energy consumption and risk of environmental pollution, which are also of concern regarding the maintenance of gates. In addition, the issues of fish migration and salinity control have globally been discussed.
