AB  - Concrete is one of the most widely used construction materials in the world and consists of up to 80% of fine and coarse aggregates obtained from river/sea beds and quarries. However, a lot of construction and demolition waste is generated annually that could be recycled as aggregate. For now, this is only done on a limited scale, which is unfortunate because full implementation of this recycling option holds significant benefits, that is, reduction of resource depletion, land use, waste and greenhouse gas emissions, etc. The extent of these benefits is confirmed by a life cycle assessment (LCA). This chapter presents a literature review on expected environmental impacts inherent to production and use of recycled aggregates and other common concrete constituents, as well as a LCA concerning some key issues when dealing with recycled aggregate concrete (e.g., functional unit, system boundaries, attributional versus consequential LCA, and transport).
