AB  - During the last decade, fiber-reinforced polymer (FRP) composite materials have been advocated as an alternative to steel and concrete by infrastructure owners for extending the life of existing structures and increasing the durability of new structures due to the advantages they offer over conventional materials. Considering the wide variability of application, this chapter focuses on application of composites in bridge structure applications. Due to their noncorrosive nature, light weight, and reduced rehabilitation time, owners have been increasingly considering these materials during the last decade. Due to increased emphasis on life-cycle costs for effective bridge management and development of design and construction specifications, these materials have potential to gain more use and acceptability in the future. At the same time, structural health monitoring (SHM) is emerging as another tool, as bridge owners are more and more comfortable using SHM to make decisions related to bridge management activities. As FRP components can be prefabricated in controlled environments and thus have potential to be instrumented with appropriate sensors ahead of the field installation, SHM has great potential for use in a complementary fashion with composite materials. This chapter briefly describes this complementary relationship with case studies.
