AB  - Publisher Summary
The life cycle assessment of hydrogen production is, in the cases where conventional fuels are converted into hydrogen, similar to many existing studies, in that major impacts are found to derive from air pollution and global warming issues. This chapter provides an insight into the social implications of using fuel cell technology for power generation. It provides an understanding of life-cycle analysis and assessment and illustrates a number of examples of the application of lifecycle analysis to hydrogen systems. Based on this, it brings about a discussion of environmental impacts from each component of the hydrogen systems, as well as other impacts that have been identified. Under the analysis of life-cycle, this chapter focuses on one of direct conversion of biomass to hydrogen, aimed to clarify the claim that such hydrogen production pathways will reduce impacts. Although many of the technologies remain emerging, there are important implications from the generic assessment that can be made at present. For fuel cell conversion of hydrogen, the industrial progress is such that meaningful lifecycle studies can start to be made on a fairly detailed basis. A concrete example of this is described in this chapter.
