AB  - Development of “green” concrete, with a significantly reduced environmental burden across a range of impact categories, is an important step towards a sustainable construction industry. Industrial by-products, such as fly ash (from coal combustion) and silica fume (from silicon metal production) or blast-furnace slag (from steel production) can be used to replace (large) portions of the ordinary Portland cement, the traditional binder component of concrete. When assessing the sustainability of fly ash (and silica fume) or blast-furnace slag concrete, care should be taken to evaluate their environmental impact properly. Preferably, life cycle assessment (LCA) is used to achieve this goal. Therefore, a suitable functional unit needs to be defined. This reference unit should take into account the differences in strength and service life with the reference concrete. A useful functional unit could be the required concrete volume per unit of strength and service life or a steel-reinforced concrete slab with a predefined mechanical load and lifespan. Attention should also go to the allocation of impacts associated with fly ash, silica fume, and blast-furnace slag. Analyses using the problem-oriented CML-IA impact method show that the use of the industrial by-products can be much less environmentally friendly, when their impacts are allocated by economic value (fly ash: 1%; silica fume: 4.8%; blast-furnace slag: 2.3%). In terms of carbon footprinting, the benefits of concrete with high cement replacement levels may still be significant, but this may not be the case anymore for (eco)toxicity, acidification, and eutrophication. A lot depends though on the local availability and the resulting economic value of the by-products. The incorporation of 30kg of chemically treated flax fibers in the concrete with high cement replacement levels does not impose important additional environmental burdens for 9 out of 10 CML-IA baseline impact categories. Only in terms of terrestrial ecotoxicity does there seem to be a major problem as the fibers comprise 68% of the overall impact. This is induced by the extensive use of fertilizers to cultivate the flax. However, if an important service life extension with inclusion of those fibers could be confirmed, their use would probably still be justified from an environmental point of view.
