AB  - Publisher Summary
This chapter encompasses an extensive review of synchronous motor drives that includes different converter-machine configurations, control topologies, and feedback signal estimation, and discussion on sensorless control. Synchronous motor drives are more efficient and expensive. They have higher power density and lower rotor inertia, particularly with high-energy permanent magnet. The power factor can be programmable-leading, lagging, or unity with field control. In addition, the converter will be less expensive with unity power factor. Broadly, the machines are classified into wound-field, permanent magnet, and reluctance types. Currently, synchronous motor drives are close competitors of induction motor drives in many industrial applications, and their applications are witnessing continuous growth. Certain applications are uniquely suited for synchronous motor drives, which are generally more expensive than induction motor drives, but have higher efficiency that tends to lower the life-cycle costs. A number of practical applications of these drives, include rolling mill drive with voltage-fed converters, icebreaker ship propulsion with cycloconverter drive, gearless cement mill drive with cycloconverter, dual mining ore-crushing drive with cycloconverter, cruise ship propulsion with load-commutated current-fed converters, and future ship propulsion with high-temperature superconducting (HTS) motors.
