AB  - More and more clay brick buildings are demolished, resulting in a considerable amount of brick waste. An effective way to reuse brick waste is to use it as a supplementary cementitious material. This study proposes the joint utilization of recycled brick powder and limestone powder as a partial substitute for Portland cement and explores their synergistic effect. The results indicate that the ternary blended mixtures show better compressive strength development from 3d to 28d compared to binary blended mixtures with addition of either of the two powder materials. The pore structure is optimized by the combination of limestone powder and brick powder, while the addition of brick powder or limestone powder alone increases the proportion of harmful pores (>200 nm). Moreover, the combination of limestone powder and brick powder can accelerate the hydration of cement, promote the reaction of brick powder and generate more Mc phases. Life cycle assessment shows that the combination of limestone powder and brick powder in the cementitious system can reduce CO2 emissions and energy consumption both by up to 30 % at a similar strength level, showing prospects for more sustainable cementitious materials.
