AB  - This paper presents an experimental investigation of the specimen size effect on steel fiber reinforced alkali-activated concrete (SFR-AAC), focusing on the effects of fiber type, volume fraction and aspect ratio. Three types of steel fibers (SFs) (i.e., straight, hooked-end and corrugated fiber), three volume fractions (i.e., 0.6 %, 0.9 % and 1.2 %), and three aspect ratios (i.e., 40, 60 and 80), are respectively selected as the main variables. A total of 192 cube specimens with four different specimen sizes (i.e., 70.7, 100, 150 and 200 mm), were selected to determine the compressive and splitting tensile strengths of SFR-AAC. The results indicate that the size effect in the mechanical characteristics of SFR-AAC is positively correlated with the fiber volume fraction, but negatively correlated for the fiber aspect ratio. The corrugated SFR-AAC exhibits the greatest size effect, while the straight SFR-AAC shows the lowest. As the specimen size increases, the fiber dispersion coefficient in SFR-AAC almost keeps a constant, except that the distribution density has a decreasing trend. Based on the experimental results, the calculation formulae for the size effect laws of SFR-AAC using three classical size effect laws are established. It is proven that the Weibull size effect law achieves the best prediction result, and using it, the critical size and critical strength of SFR-AAC are obtained. In addition, the size conversion coefficients are suggested for the compressive and splitting tensile strengths of SFR-AAC. Finally, a life cycle assessment (LCA) approach is applied to calculate the carbon emission of SFR-AAC, and clarify SFR-AAC as a low-carbon material compared with steel fiber reinforced concrete (SFRC).
