AB  - Fibre-reinforced polymers (FRP) have emerged as viable substitutes for conventional steel materials because of their ability to avoid chloride-induced corrosion. However, the applicability and feasibility of FRP in concrete columns have yet to be fully elucidated. To overcome this research gap, this study incorporated carbon FRP mesh fabric (CFRP-MF) as stirrups and steel-FRP composite bar (SFCB) as longitudinal reinforcements. This study investigated the impact of the reinforcement type (steel, CFRP strips, and CFRP-MF), volume stirrup ratio, and concrete strength on the axial compression performance of concrete columns. The findings revealed that CFRP-MF enhanced the restraint capacity of the concrete core by approximately 11 % compared to conventional materials, improving the intensive restraining effect of CFRP-MF stirrups by weakening the concrete arching effect. Moreover, the load-carrying capacity of concrete column is directly proportional to stirrup volume ratio and concrete strength. Furthermore, the FRP partially constrained concrete calculation models were compared with FRP-MF, in terms of short-term life cycle impacts and structural performances. The results indicate that concrete columns with CFRP-MF stirrups and longitudinal SFCBs exhibit excellent properties, therefore establishing their potential for extensive utilization and enhanced durability in coastal environments.
