AB  - This study presents a comprehensive evaluation of recycled concrete aggregate (RCA) as a sustainable alternative in concrete construction, focusing on its mechanical performance and durability in Reinforced Cement Concrete (RCC) applications. Through a series of laboratory experiments and analyses, varying percentages of RCA substitution for conventional aggregates were investigated, encompassing compressive, tensile, and flexural strength tests, alongside assessments of workability and fresh concrete properties. Durability characteristics, including freeze–thaw resistance, chloride ion penetration resistance, sulfate attack resistance, and carbonation resistance, were also examined. The study sheds light on the optimal replacement percentages for both coarse and fine aggregate fractions, with microstructural analysis providing insights into RCA-cementitious matrix interactions. Economic and environmental assessments quantified cost savings, waste reduction, and energy conservation associated with RCA utilization, supplemented by a life cycle assessment (LCA) to gauge overall environmental impact. These findings offer practical guidelines for designing RCA-based RCC mixtures, aiming to reduce the construction industry’s environmental footprint and promote circular economy practices. Dissemination of these results is crucial for informing industry stakeholders, engineers, and policymakers about the potential of RCA as a sustainable material, facilitating the development of more eco-friendly and durable RCC structures.
