AB  - The recycling of waste, byproducts, and surplus materials is one of the cornerstones for obtaining a high level of circular economy. Owing to the increased demand for recycling of nearly all material types, insufficient environmental performance may often occur. It is evident that the assessment of the environmental suitability of the different materials needs to include the fundamental leaching properties, which are not captured by the existing models for life cycle assessments. Safe and sound leaching assessment that is valid for a circular material life may be performed by a systematic characterization of the basic leaching properties for single and combined materials in monolithic and in a granular condition using standardized test protocols. The leaching characteristics can further be clarified by geochemical speciation modeling, including mechanisms such as sorption, aqueous complex formation, mineral dissolution/precipitation, solid solution formation, change in redox conditions, and carbonation. Existing software modeling frameworks (e.g., LeachXS) have capabilities to describe these processes. Significant added values can be gained by using a fundamental approach such as identification of critical and beneficial material combinations, comparison to existing criteria, and assessment of the long-term leaching behavior.
