AB  - This study aims to systematically investigate the interface properties of FRP-ECC-concrete composites with shear testing, mechanical model, and life-cycle assessment. The interface roughness between ECC and concrete was made by 3D printing pre-grooving method. The mechanical properties of composites interface were conducted via single-side shear test, where investigated parameters included interface roughness, ECC thickness, and ECC compressive strength. Three failure modes of FRP-ECC-concrete composites interface were observed. The results indicated that all investigated parameters have significant effects on shear strength and failure mode of FRP-ECC-concrete composites interface. Both ECC compressive strength and interface roughness are positively correlated with interface shear strength, while ECC thickness is negatively correlated. More importantly, an innovative mechanical model was proposed, which can simultaneously predict interface failure modes and interface shear strength. Its prediction results were in good agreement with measured results of both this study and other research. In addition, a life-cycle assessment was conducted to evaluate economic and environmental impacts of the use of FRP-ECC composites for strengthening and 3D printing interface pre-grooving method. This study is expected to promote a more comprehensive understanding of interface properties of FRP-ECC-concrete composites and application of FRP-ECC composites for concrete strengthening and repairs.
