AB  - Road construction is a significant activity that consumes natural resources. Therefore, replacing a portion of natural aggregate (NA) with the recycled concrete aggregate (RCA) will be a win–win option for reducing natural resource consumption and solving the waste disposal problem. The efficient aggregate modification method can improve the use rate of recycled aggregate and the durability of recycled aggregate asphalt mixture roads. This report summarizes the findings of the existing research on modified RCA (MRCA) for asphalt mixture pavement, in general, and hot mix asphalt (HMA), in particular. Methods of modification include mechanical, chemical, and biological treatment. The performance difference between RCA and NA, andthe impact of different modification methods on the performance improvement of RCA, were investigated, and the road performance of HMA containing various MRCA was compared. In addition, the impact of several modification methods on the chemical and physical properties of RCA, and the surface contact have been discussed. According to these findings, it is suggested that the construction of modified aggregate roads should be encouraged, life cycle assessments (LCA) be performed and more consistent standard specifications and recommendations for the usage of modified aggregate roads should be produced.
