AB  - Green concrete is a concrete which can be produced from waste material as at least one of its components and ensures life-cycle sustainability.In order to preserve natural resources by dropping excessive exploitation of rocks for aggregate production, researchers are intended to predict alternate aggregates towards developing green concrete for sustainable development of construction industry. This paper provides the results obtained from an investigation which was carried out using waste building block, namely Autoclaved Aerated Concrete (AAC) brick as aggregate for developing lightweight concrete (LWC). The property variations both in fresh and hardened states were studied using M20 and M30 grade normal strength concrete. The effect of AAC brick trash on concrete properties such as slump, density and variations in mechanical properties were determined and presented. The AAC brick trash was used under saturated condition and it was replaced the granite aggregate by 10 %, 20 %, 30 %, 40 %, 50 % and 60 %. The mechanical properties were conducted after the curing period of 7, 28 and 90 days. Ordinary Portland cement (OPC), Portland pozzolana cement (PPC-fly ash based) and Portland slag cement (PSC) were used in this investigation to evaluate the compatibility of AAC brick trash with cement paste. From the investigation, it was found that the ideal replacement of AAC brick trash was obtained to develop the green LWC by replacing the granite aggregate by 30 %, 31 % and 29.5 % while using OPC, PSC and PPC respectively, with reference to the density and mechanical properties of LWC.
