AB  - In addition to providing the required leak tightness in normal conditions, reactor containment structures and buildings are designed to withstand loadings that may result from operational accidents: in short, they provide at all times the last barrier to prevent discharges of radioactive substances to the external environment. Both external (e.g., earthquakes and hurricanes) and internal events (e.g., pressurized line breaks) are considered in reactor designs. Age-related deterioration of concrete containment structures must be controlled to ensure required leak tightness and integrity under all foreseen conditions. Structural safety margins, however, may be reduced by aging and environmental impacts. Besides the reactor containment, other concrete structures (e.g., spent fuel pools, biological shields, and cooling towers) are important to plant safety, operation, or economics as they provide support, shielding, and containment functions: all these structures are exposed to the adverse impacts of water, high temperatures, and radiation fields. The service life for most reactors was initially 30–40 years. However, over time a number of factors have led utilities to prolong plant service life up to 60–80 years: life extension, delayed construction schedules, and/or deferred decommissioning strategies, all reflect in integrity requirements for nuclear concrete structures lasting well over 150 years and possibly approaching 200 years. The longest phase in a nuclear plant lifecycle is generally decommissioning; two strategic variants of deferred decommissioning are safe enclosure and entombment, and this chapter will focus on long-term issues affecting cementitious material during the implementation of these strategies. Concrete is a long-lasting material and its containment functions over several decades of plant operations have been good. However, experience shows that aging, often caused or enhanced by poor quality materials, exposure to extreme environments, abnormal structural loads, etc., can reduce the safety functions of concrete structures and increase risks to workers and the public. Effective aging management of concrete structures is required to ensure continuing safety functions throughout plant service life and decommissioning. Several concrete management programs, such as periodic inspection, testing, surveillance, and preventive/corrective maintenance, are underway at many nuclear installations.
