AB  - When a concrete-filled steel tube (CFST) is used in offshore construction such as electricity towers, bridges or platforms, it is subjected to continuous degradation during its service life due to the long-term in-service actions and the ocean corrosive environment. Fibre reinforced polymer (FRP) strengthening makes an effective solution to extending the service life of corroded CFST columns in existing offshore structures. For the rational design and tactful application of this corrosion strengthening technique, it is crucially important to understand the life-cycle performance of the strengthened column after experiencing the combination of multiple deterioration factors. In this study, an advanced finite element analysis (FEA) model, which is stage-by-stage verified against collected test data on 104 columns, is established to capture the nonlinear structural performance of FRP-strengthened CFST columns under coupled progressive degradation factors, i.e., initial imperfections, construction preload, sustained service load and corrosion. Corrosion of the steel results in less confinement on concrete and thus affects the loading capacity of the columns, while increasing the number of FRP layers can weaken the negative influence brought by multiple degradation factors. Based on parametric studies, a simplified design method is proposed for the ultimate strength prediction of corroded CFST columns strengthened by FRP.
