AB  - The rapid urban construction and industrial growth have led to the accumulation of significant amounts of recycled concrete powder and calcium carbide slag, causing environmental pollution and resource waste. This study explores the use of these materials in producing low-heat cement (LHC) clinker, aiming to enhance waste recycling, conserve natural resources, and reduce the carbon footprint of cement production. We produced clinker with a composition of KH 0.67–0.76, IM 0.94, and SM 2.81, achieving standard-compliant f-CaO content when fired at 1350 ℃. The primary clinker phases were C2S, C3S, C3A, and C4AF, with C3S2 forming at 1300 ℃ in the KH 0.67 group, transitioning to C2S at higher temperatures. Hydration resulted in double-peak heat release curves, with AFm and Ca(OH)2 as main products. Under optimal conditions (KH 0.76, SM 2.81, IM 0.94), the mineral types remained consistent. Life cycle assessment showed significant carbon emission reductions: from 914.20 kg to 376.71 kg for Ordinary Portland Cement (OPC) clinker (58.80 %) and from 883.51 kg to 374.44 kg for low-heat clinker (57.62 %).
