AB  - The manufacturing of Ordinary Portland Cement (OPC) is responsible for 10% of global carbon emissions. As an alternative to OPC, an alkaline residue from industrial processes (e.g., stainless steel slag or SSS) can be mixed with alkali activator solutions to produce alternative construction material. At present, the real environmental consequences of the introduction of SSS-based blocks into the market for construction materials are not fully understood. Moreover, the introduction of a new product into a well-established market can have indirect effects (e.g., rebound effect) that must be considered when evaluating the environmental consequences. The chapter aims at analyzing the environmental consequence when introducing new recycled product into a well-established market, focusing on the production of SSS blocks with a focus on the Flemish market (in Belgium). The environmental analysis is performed by using a Consequential Life Cycle Assessment (CLCA). The study identifies the market for nonstructural construction blocks and the market for low-quality aggregates as the market affected by the production of SSS blocks. The recycling of SSS as an alternative cement for building materials will reduce the impact thanks to the avoided use of traditional construction blocks (OPC concrete or ceramic bricks). At the same time the change in the final use of SSS causes an increased use of natural aggregates for low-value applications, unless alternatives from other residues are considered. The CLCA approach indicates that replacing a material by a more sustainable option does not automatically lead to a better overall sustainability, since the markets and the marginal suppliers affected by the substitution must also be considered.
