AB  - Self-healing of concrete is an ability to undergo a healing mechanism on its own due to the hydration of cement in the vicinity of cracks, which are formed in structures upon loading or due to weathering. Hence it requires little maintenance. Normally the cracks in concrete grow at a steady state due to inappropriate loading or environmental exposure. Due to poor maintenance, these cracks aggravate and reduce the load-carrying capacity of the structure. Consequently, the maintenance works raise the life cycle cost of the structure. Hence, concrete of grade M40 is prepared as per Indian Standards to study the hybrid effect of Polyvinyl alcohol, sodium bentonite, Nano silica and Rice husk ash on the self-healing ability of concrete. This study is carried by varying Polyvinyl alcohol in the proportions of 0 wt%, 0.5 wt%, 1 wt%, 1.5 wt% and 2 wt% with optimum proportions of rice husk ash, Nano Silica, and Sodium Bentonite as 10 wt%, 1 wt%, and 6 wt% respectively. The concrete samples are tested for compression and flexure on 7th and 28th days of the curing period. The concrete prisms are restrained using end plates connected with a rod along its axis to avoid fracture under flexure. The prisms are pre-loaded until the cracks were formed. The crack widths are then measured using a handy microscope. To study the closure of cracks after a healing period of 28 days, Digital image processing is carried out using MATLAB software.
