AB  - In this study, the hydration mechanism and environmental impacts of blended cements with the co-combustion ash of rice husk and sewage sludge (CCA) were investigated and compared to those of blended cements with sewage sludge ash (SSA). CCA possesses lower phosphate contents than SSA, leading to lower inhibition effects on early hydration of cement clinker. Moreover, the pozzolanic activity of CCA is higher than that of SSA. Thus, more hydration products from the pozzolanic reaction of CCA are generated in CCA-based blended cements. Compared to the matrix of SSA-based blended cements, that of their CCA-based counterpart is filled with more hydration products, which promotes porosity refinement and strength development of CCA-based blended cements at later ages. CCA-based blended cements exhibit greater environmental benefits than SSA-based blended cements because fossil consumption and toxic substance emissions during the co-combustion of rice husk and sewage sludge is lower than that during the mono-combustion of sewage sludge.
