AB  - Cement concrete is said to be a versatile material but its sustainability is always questioned. There exists a huge scope to improve the sustainability of traditional concrete as its primary binding ingredient i.e., cement is responsible for a huge amount of carbon dioxide production. Since now-a-day footpaths, roads and parking etc. places are being renovated and constructed with concrete paver blocks instead of traditional methods like flexible pavements this also has increased concrete usage. Paver block making concrete is very different from structural concrete owing to the ratio of fine aggregate to coarse aggregate as per Indian code’s provisions and since there is a difference in composition, there will be difference in ecological impacts. In India, various industrial by-products are being generated out of which fly ash and bagasse ash are two of many, which are produced during the electricity generation in the plants. There exists a perennial problem associated with the disposal issue of these materials. Life cycle assessment is a method that is used to evaluate the ecological impacts of the materials where impacts can be evaluated at midpoint levels as well as endpoint levels. In this study, four types of concretes e.g., traditional concrete, fly ash-based blended concrete and two geopolymer concretes (one with only fly ash as precursor, second with binary blend of fly ash and sugarcane bagasse as precursor) were prepared for the production of paver blocks and comparison of their ecological impacts thereof which was studied at the endpoint damage categories. Results from this study showed that cement-based traditional concrete blocks were much more harmful as compared to other concrete blocks in all the damage categories.
