AB  - Concrete production significantly contributes to the construction industry's carbon emissions and resource depletion. Replacing Portland cement with supplementary cementitious materials (SCMs) can improve the sustainability and performance of concrete. However, limited holistic research compares SCMs considering environmental, economic, durability, and strength criteria. This study comprehensively evaluates supplementary cementitious materials (SCMs) in concrete production using life cycle assessment. The SCMs examined were rice husk ash (RHA), sugarcane bagasse ash (SCBA), metakaolin (MK), and trass. The study quantified CO2 emissions, cost, compressive strength, and service life of concrete mixes with these SCMs and compared them to ordinary Portland cement concrete. The findings reveal that the 15 % metakaolin replacement sample had the best overall performance. It reduced CO2 emissions of one m3 of concrete per year of service life by 83 % and costs of one m3 per year by 78 % compared to ordinary concrete. The metakaolin concrete also increased the compressive strength by 25 % and extended the service life from 8 to 43 years in a marine environment. Among the SCMs, RHA demonstrated a 90 % reduction in CO2 emissions per service life year and unit compressive strength compared to ordinary concrete. SCBA had the highest cost due to high production requirements. Trass offered a 44 % decrease in CO2 emissions per service life year and unit compressive strength. The results highlight metakaolin's optimal balance between sustainability and performance. The reduced CO2 emissions demonstrate SCMs' potential to lower the construction industry's carbon footprint. The study offers original data and insights to enable greener and lower carbon concrete production using supplementary cementitious materials.
