AB  - Accelerated carbonation is an innovative technology for enhancing cement-based materials as well as mitigating CO2 emissions. The present article comprehensively summarizes the research status, relative mechanisms and application effect of accelerated carbonation in cement-based materials life cycle. The accelerated carbonation methods and strategies for enhancing carbonation efficiency are fully discussed. CO2 mitigation potential, research gaps and future perspectives of this technology are systematically analyzed. Results showed that, through carbonation reactions, cement-based wastes could be utilized to extract high-purity calcium carbonates, and valorized as high-quality recycled aggregates. Compared with traditional curing methods, CO2 curing could promote materials to achieve superior compressive strength and durability due to the formation of calcium carbonate. In addition, the application scenarios, carbonation conditions and advantages in industry practice of standard, pressurized and flow-through accelerated carbonation were distinct. Liquid-solid carbonation, high temperature, pretreatment with calcium-rich solutions were suggested as efficient ways for enhancing carbonation efficiency. Moreover, CO2 sequestration and the reduction of cement addition contributed to CO2 mitigation. This review provides valuable insights into recent advances and future perspectives on the application of accelerated carbonation in the concrete industry.
