AB  - The development of ferrochrome slag-containing construction materials, and the investigation of their mechanical and the contaminant leaching behaviour have received consideration research attention. However, few comparative studies have investigated the mechanical and contaminant leaching of fired versus cement-based construction materials incorporating metallurgical slag. Therefore, the current paper investigated and compared both the mechanical and contaminant leaching behaviour of cement-based and fired bricks containing metallurgical ferrochrome slag to their corresponding conventional bricks (controls). To simulate the typical cradle-to-grave life cycle of construction materials, the contaminant leaching behaviour was tracked in raw materials, brick monoliths, and construction debris. Cement-based and fired clay bricks containing 20–30% ferrochrome slag had significantly higher compressive strength and lower water absorption than conventional bricks (controls). In both monolith and debris forms, the slag-containing bricks had similar or lower contaminant (trace metals, chloride) leaching than the controls. The only exceptions were Mn and Cr which showed higher leaching in slag-containing brick debris than conventional bricks, probably due to the relatively higher initial concentrations of Cr and Mn in the slag. Cement stabilization gave the highest strength properties whereas firing in clay bricks resulted in the least leaching of contaminants. Overall, the findings demonstrate that metallurgical ferrochrome slag can replace 20–30% of sand or clay in cement-based and fired clay bricks, respectively, and the risk of contaminant leaching in slag-containing bricks was below the maximum guidelines for hazardous wastes. The conversion of slag into construction materials is attractive, and serves a dual function: (1) minimizes the pollution risks associated with current improper disposal practices, and (2) provides a valued-added industrial product with a putatively lower environmental footprint than those based on pristine raw materials. Further work should focus on: (1) life cycle assessment, and (2) long-term mechanical and leaching behaviour at a pilot scale.
