AB  - The aim of this paper is to determine and evaluate the environmental impacts of the production of EPS-based products complemented through a discussion on the use and end-of-life processing stages of these materials. Firstly, an innovative “cradle to gate” Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) of the environmental impacts resulting from the production of EPS granulates (virgin, regranulated and regranulated additivated), lightweight concrete with EPS, and high-density EPS boards, based on site-specific data, is presented. Then, a complementary “cradle to cradle” LCA discussion is presented, including the following additional life cycle stages: maintenance and replacement (B2–B4), operational energy use (B6), and end-of-life stage, including the benefits and loads beyond the system boundary (C1–C4 and D). The “cradle to gate” results were based on site-specific data from Portuguese factories and resulted from a solid methodology that is fully described, including the raw materials extraction and production, and the modelling of energy and transport processes at the production stage of these materials. These results reflect the weight of the raw materials within the production process of the EPS-based products studied in almost all environmental categories, and show that some life cycle stages, such as transportation of raw materials (A2) and packaging and packaging waste (A3.1 and A3.3, respectively), may not be discarded in a “cradle to gate” study of a construction material, because they can make a significant contribution to some environmental categories.
