AB  - Recently, a successful application of recycled aggregate concrete (RAC) block building in practical engineering has been implemented at Nanjing Tech University. This chapter analyzes and compares the environmental impact assessment of concrete building blocks manufactured with recycled concrete aggregates (RCAs)—RAC blocks, using life-cycle assessment (LCA) methodology. Five different environmental impact categories [i.e., global warming potential (GWP), acidification potential (AP), eutrophication potential (EP), human toxicity potential (HTP), and photochemical fog in the atmosphere potential (POCP)] are calculated and comparatively analyzed as well as to analyze transportation distance sensitivity. The results of LCA case studies in the production of RAC blocks and the effect of RCAs on the environmental performance are presented. The LCA results indicate that the environmental impacts from the production of RAC blocks are less than that of normal concrete blocks. The use of construction and demolition (C&D) waste to produce concrete building blocks has several advantages and significantly improves the environmental footprint and sustainable development of masonry structures. At last, recommendations for further study are proposed.
