AB  - Sustainable well completions and integrity become critical in response to the rapidly increasing growth in oil and gas production brought about by unconventional drilling. Ensuring durable zonal isolation behind casing is essential to prevent leakage and safeguard subsurface inter-zone communications, critical for overall well integrity. The longevity and safety of underground gas storage hinge on the performance of the cement employed in well completions. Class G cement represents more desirable alternatives to conventional Portland cements, but unreconciled deficiencies pose risks to its reliable application, particularly in highly volatile hydrocarbon well completions and underground gas storage. This study delves into the synergistic effects of fly ash and eggshell powder (FAESP) on Class G cement performance, aiming to enhance its mechanical strength, durability, and resistance to the extreme downhole environments encountered in oil and gas fields wells. Fly ash, a well-recognized pozzolanic material, and eggshell powder, a bio-based additive, were strategically chosen for their potential to improve cement properties and contribute to sustainable well completions for underground gas storage. The materials, including Class G cement powder, fly ash, eggshell powder and ilmenite were characterized using XRF analysis, particle size distribution and SEM-EDX analysis. A comprehensive set of tests were conducted to evaluate the effects of varying concentrations of fly ash and eggshell powder (FAESP) ranging from 0.25% to 4.0% by weight of cement (BWOC) on class G cement and benchmarked against plain class G cement (Base cement). The study encompassed a series of tests which include rheological parameters; compressive and tensile strengths; elastic attributes (Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio); and petrophysical characteristics such as porosity and permeability assessment, simulating the harsh reservoir conditions. The results indicated that the optimum fly ash (FA) and eggshell powder (ESP) concentration was 2% by weight of cement (BWOC), based on wellbore-relevant performance tests. This concentration significantly improved yield stress and gel strength while reducing porosity and permeability, addressing gas migration concerns. It also enhanced compressive strength and imparted resilience, as evidenced by a reduction in Young's Modulus. This first-of-its-kind study validates the potential for sustainable and cost-effective advancement of ilmenite-cement properties by harnessing the synergistic effects of combining fly ash and eggshell powder. The findings demonstrate the feasibility of ensuring durable zonal isolation over the well life cycle.
