AB  - In alignment with Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) set by United Nation, this study investigates eco-friendly alternatives which can be used in various construction activities. This research explores the potential of utilising recycled concrete fine aggregates (RCFA) and Ceramic Insulator Fine Aggregates (CIFA) as a complete substitute for natural river sand in rendering mortar applications, thoroughly examining mechanical and durability properties. The initial results demonstrated that the mortar mix made using 50% replacement of river sand with RCFA showed comparable results of compressive strength when compared with the control mix containing only river sand. However, the results of shrinkage and durability demonstrated the adverse effects of the use of RCFA in mortar with shrinkage being almost double to that in control mix. In order to enhance the performance of the mortar, the CIFA were added along with 50% RCFA. The addition of 50% CIFA along with 50% RCFA resulted in overall better performance with approximately 25% and 10% increase in compressive and adhesive strength, respectively. Additionally, the waste-incorporated mortar also outperforms the control mix in durability tests involving exposure to 5% HCL and H2SO4 solutions over 180 days. The results showed 2–3% lower mass and compressive strength loss compared to control mix indicating the better mechanical and durability performance of mortar mix. The SEM and XRD microstructure analysis techniques were also made use to further validate the mechanical and durability findings. To assess the sustainability of the mortar mixes, life cycle assessment (LCA) was conducted with a “cradle to gate” boundary condition. Furthermore, the Analytical Hierarchy Process (AHP) was employed to evaluate and rank the various mortar mixes based on their mechanical, functional (shrinkage and adhesive strength) and durability properties, along with sustainability indicator. The results of the AHP reveals that the mortar mix with 100% waste incorporation ranks first among all the other alternatives, highlighting its potential as an effective and sustainable option compared to conventional rendering mortar.
