AB  - The massive accumulation of waste oyster shells (WOSs) causes irreversible environmental problems. To alleviate the environmental burden and save the depleting natural resources, attempts have been made to use WOSs for the production of sustainable concrete. This paper first surveyed the physical, mechanical and durability properties of the WOS cement-based materials. Then, the state-of-the-art of the WOS cement-based materials in terms of thermal insulation, sound absorption, fireproof properties, as well as environmental and economic impacts was highlighted. Additionally, based on the results of the Review existing research gaps were filled. An elastic-plastic damage constitutive model with respect to the influence of WOS replacement was first proposed to better reflect the elastic/plastic behavior, mechanical damage characteristics and the influence of WOS replacement. Finally, the life cycle assessment (LCA) of WOS mortar was first performed by analyzing the replacement of fine aggregates with WOS, which integrates the impacts of 13 environmental categories. The LCA results indicated that replacing fine aggregate with WOS for mortar production reduced 9 of the 13 impact categories, while the increase in the other 4 categories was attributed to the electricity consumption of WOS pretreatment. Based on a comprehensive review and preliminary exploration of research gaps, this paper can provide reference and inspiration for researchers in the field of recycling waste materials.
