AB  - Reinforcement corrosion is identified as a major cause of premature deterioration of reinforced concrete (RC) structures and infrastructure. Coupling effects of seismic actions and material degradation may cause unpredictable failure modes and adversely affects structural behavior. To facilitate and enhance pre-earthquake and post-earthquake event mitigation and emergency response strategies, seismic fragility assessment has attracted increased attention. Life-cycle seismic fragility analysis is an effective method for quantitative evaluation of seismic performance for RC structures exposed to chloride environment. This paper presents a state-of-the-art review on seismic fragility analysis for deteriorated structures with a special focus on non-uniform corrosion and multi-uncertainty. The spatial non-uniform corrosion is classified into four different levels: material, cross-section, component and structure. Gaps and deficiencies in previous studies are outlined to guide future research.
