AB  - Plug and Abandonment (P&A) operations represent a critical phase in the life cycle of oil and gas wells when they become economically unviable and the decommissioning process begins. In this context, P&A aims to safely seal the wellbore by implementing barrier systems to avoid internal well fluid leakage into the external environment. Besides its environmental importance, barrier systems must have long-term durability and the well owner becomes responsible for monitoring and maintenance procedures. Portland cement pastes are widely used due to their well-documented material properties, and cost-effectiveness. On the other hand, resins, geopolymers, and metal-based alloys can also offer excellent performance in terms of mechanical and chemical properties, long-term durability, and competitive cost-effectiveness, but little attention is given to these materials due to the lack of information provided in the literature. However, if not properly designed it can lead to failures under high mechanical stress or hostile environmental conditions such as high temperature and high pressure (HTHP) and the presence of contaminants. In this regard, the present review aims to highlight the different materials used in P&A operations, focusing on their mechanical, chemical, and durability properties, providing readers valuable insights into the usage of different materials for challenging P&A scenarios, and allowing the understanding of their impact on operational success. Also, the practical applicability of these materials in real P&A operations and information about cost-effectiveness are discussed. Furthermore, the article provides future perspectives on new materials and highlights opportunities to advance research and technology in this field.
