AB  - To address the challenges caused by the excessive self-weight of traditional reinforcement concrete (RC) pylons and double-skinned normal concrete (DS-NC) composite pylons in large-span suspension bridges, this study introduces an innovative lightweight assembled bridge pylon comprising double-skinned ultra-high-performance concrete (DS-UHPC) composite panels. This novel pylon design aims to improve load-bearing efficiency and seismic performance while facilitating factory prefabrication and on-site assembly. Focusing on a suspension bridge with a main span of 2200 m, this paper investigates the conceptual and structural design, mechanical analysis, economic evaluation, and carbon emission assessment of the DS-UHPC composite pylon. The findings reveal that the self-weight of the DS-UHPC composite pylon is only 14 % of that of a traditional RC pylon, leading to a 37 % reduction of it in axial forces under the ultimate limit state. The reduced bending stiffness further results in a 76 % decrease in the longitudinal bending moment compared to the RC pylon. The reduction in both self-weight and bending stiffness enhances the seismic performance of the DS-UHPC composite pylon, as indicated by maximum internal forces during seismic events, which range only from 1/7 to 1/3 of those observed in the RC pylon. Although the life-cycle cost of the DS-NC composite pylon is 9 % lower and the RC pylon costs 43 % less than that of the DS-UHPC composite pylon, it offers an optimal balance between cost-efficiency and superior performance, particularly in high seismic intensity areas. The DS-UHPC composite pylon also demonstrates significant environmental advantages, with steel pylons producing 250 % more carbon emissions and RC pylons generating 37 % more emissions over their life cycle compared to the DS-UHPC composite pylon. Overall, the proposed DS-UHPC composite pylon provides a high-performance, cost-effective, and sustainable solution for large-span suspension bridge construction.
