AB  - While the addition of biochar to cement paste presents a potential method for carbon capture, the current low levels of content and unoptimized particle distribution make it difficult for CO2 to diffuse into the cementitious paste, resulting in a lower overall degree of carbonation and poor permeability. In this study, bamboo-derived biochar was utilized as a raw material, and an innovative biochar particle size distribution arranged according to a Poisson distribution and controlled by a mathematical expectation (λ) was developed. The carbonation degree and water absorption performance were tested under different levels of incorporation and water cement ratios. The results indicate that the carbon capture capacity increases with higher biochar content and water cement ratios, while showing a trend of initially decreasing and then increasing with increasing λ. Water absorption capacity also increases with higher biochar content, λ, and water cement ratios, a phenomenon effectively validated by a permeation model developed in the program. The sensitivity of carbon capture capacity and permeability to changes in λ increases with higher levels of biochar incorporation and lower water cement ratios. Furthermore, it was found that the pore structure network formed by biochar in the cementitious matrix serves as a channel for transport and storage of substances (such as CO2, water, etc). The interfacial transition zone (ITZ) acts as a resistance barrier for substance transport within the pores. Increasing biochar content and water cement ratios enhance the connectivity of the pore structure network and improves pore compatibility of the ITZ, leading to higher levels of carbonation and water absorption capacity in the cement paste. Additionally, environmental assessments demonstrate that increasing biochar content and λ can effectively reduce greenhouse gas emissions and energy consumption in the product. Multi-criteria evaluations reveal that a water cement ratio of 0.40, biochar content, and λ of 40 % and 3.0 respectively, exhibit the optimal overall performance. This offers a green and low-carbon alternative construction material for sustainable infrastructure.
