AB  - Insufficient knowledge of the structure and properties of pumice-based earth concrete limits its potential for use in the construction industry. This study offers new insights into the utilization of pumice as a natural pozzolan in Self-Compacting Earth Concrete (SCEC). The aim of this study is to investigate the effect of pumice on the physical, mechanical and microstructural properties, as well as the Life Cycle Analysis (LCA) of Self-Compacting Earth Concrete (SCEC). For this purpose, compressive strength tests were first conducted on pastes with 50 %, 60 %, 70 %, and 80 % earth substitution of three earth types with cement to identify the optimal earth. Then, SCEC mixtures with optimal earth and varying pumice contents (10 %, 15 %, 25 %, and 35 %) were evaluated in fresh and hardened states. Results show that pumice incorporation improves the fresh state and reduces superplasticizer usage. Also, the compressive strength of mixtures containing 15 % pumice remains close to the ones of the SCEC mixture with 50 % earth at long-term ages. Additionally, pumice-containing SCEC demonstrates long-term improvement in ultrasonic pulse velocity (UPV) and density properties, with increased CH and C-S-H production and reduced ettringite based on microstructure analyses. Findings reveal that incorporating 15 % pumice into SCEC can decrease the total environmental weight and CO2 emissions by 48.73 % and 52.93 %, respectively, compared to the reference mixture. This research suggests that pumice-based SCEC has potential as an innovative green concrete for the construction industry.
