AB  - Demolition is a critical process in the life cycle of concrete structures. In alignment with low-carbon policies and environmental requirements, soundless chemical demolition technology (SCDT) has been presented to demolish concrete structure. Given that the single-hole damage was the basis for understanding the demolition process induced by SCDT, this study investigated the influence of two important parameters hole diameter and hole depth on the damage degree of SCDT demolished single-hole concrete blocks. The results demonstrated that the hole diameter showcased more pronounced influence on the demolition efficiency, as an increase in hole diameter effectively shortened cracking time and increased both total crack width and fractal dimension. However, the hole depth showed more significant effect within a certain range, when the hole depth was less than 60 mm, the increase in hole depth had a substantial impact, while beyond this depth, the impact diminished. The residual compressive bearing capacity was used to validate the feasibility of employing total crack widths and fractal dimension as indicators for evaluating damage, and the hole design parameters regulated demolition efficiency was further clarified. This study provides foundational insights into the damage behavior of single-hole concrete blocks, offering inspiration for the high-efficiency demolition of concrete structures.
