AB  - Recycled aggregate concrete (RAC) plays a significant role in the circular economy and sustainable development of the construction industry by effectively reducing raw material consumption, promoting resource efficiency, and facilitating the low-carbon transition of the sector. Based on 1255 RAC mix design data collected from published literature and laboratory experiments, this study employs machine learning methods to develop an intelligent prediction model for RAC compressive strength and designs an intelligent mix proportioning model to meet target compressive strengths, resulting in a dataset containing millions of mix designs across various compressive strength grades. The Maximum Information Coefficient (MIC) method is used to analyze the relationships between RAC compressive strength and various influencing factors. Additionally, a carbon emission calculation model for RAC is constructed using the life cycle assessment (LCA) method, and carbon emissions across different strength grades of RAC are analyzed. Sustainable design target values for RAC are established, considering the critical transportation distance of recycled aggregates. The study further explores the potential of emerging road transport technologies to enhance the application of recycled aggregates. The results indicate that recycled aggregate (RA) and the water-to-cement ratio are the most critical factors affecting RAC compressive strength. RAC mix design optimization demonstrates significant potential for carbon emission reduction, particularly with a notable impact from the transportation phase. Emerging road transport technologies could facilitate the broader use of RA, especially in low-strength concrete, to maximize resource recycling and reduce carbon emissions. The findings provide both theoretical and practical support for the recycling and application of RA in concrete.
