AB  - Carbon sequestration technology has emerged as a pivotal strategy for attaining the "dual carbon" goals, amidst the persistent rise in global carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions. In the field of concrete technology, carbon sequestration strategies can be categorized into two main approaches. One approach involves reacting natural ores and industrial waste with carbon dioxide to incorporate them into concrete, while the other ensures the stable and long-term storage of CO2 throughout the entire life cycle of concrete, from initial preparation to maintenance and final reuse. The basic mechanism involves the reaction between CO2, calcium and magnesium components in concrete, leading to the formation of calcium carbonate. Carbon sequestration strategies, spanning from carbon capture at the raw material stage to the entire concrete production process, are introduced comprehensively. A detailed analysis is conducted on the characteristics, intrinsic mechanisms, and carbon sequestration potential of various carbon sequestration technologies. Additionally, the tool CiteSpace is used to help readers gain an in-depth understanding of the research status and future development trends in the field of carbon solidified concrete. Specifically, natural minerals, alkaline industrial wastes, and recycled aggregates enhance concrete performance by filling pores, improving interfacial bonding, and promoting the reaction of unhydrated cement particles with CO2 to form calcium carbonate. Furthermore, it is pointed out that in order to promote the mineralization reaction, the regulations of concrete curing and mixing stage should be further improved, the compatibility between microorganisms and concrete should be improved, and to find new materials and new methods to promote the innovation of concrete carbon fixation technology. Moreover, various critical factors influencing carbon sequestration efficiency are examined, including the composition and particle size characteristics of the materials, as well as external conditions such as reaction time, temperature pressure and concentration of CO2, moisture content, pH, etc. Additionally, carbon sequestration can be evaluated using numerical calculation methods, such as weighing, physical analysis, chemical analysis, etc. The behavior of concrete to absorb and store CO2 can be significantly enhanced through advancements in current technologies, the development of innovative materials, and modifications to structural design. As a result, this advancement will establish a foundation for a sustainable construction industry while contributing to the reduction of greenhouse gas emissions.
