AB  - Building demolition waste has a significant EI, including waste generation, CO₂ emissions and land use alteration. Recycling and reutilizing these materials possess the potential to alleviate the strain on landfills and diminish the dependence on natural resources. Recycled aggregates from demolished structures were used to produce concrete masonry blocks. The mechanical characteristics were evaluated, and the EI assessment was carried out using SimaPro software in combination with the Ecoinvent 3.10 database, employing Impact 2002 as the selected assessment method. Incorporating 25 % recycled aggregates into masonry blocks resulted in a 1.8 % decrease in compressive strength. Subsequently, as incorporation increased to 50 %, 75 %, and 100 %, it exhibited a consistent decreasing pattern, with reductions of 2.9 %, 10.9 %, and 16.4 %, respectively. Ultrasonic pulse velocity results showed a replicating trend with compressive strength. At 100 % incorporation, EI decreased: CAR to 5.9 % and RI to 7.3 %. Overall emissions declined, with ODP and TAC decreasing by up to 5.7 % and 4.8 % respectively. Moreover, emissions decreased by 3.9 % GWP and 6.9 % for NRE when masonry blocks were produced without natural aggregates. Damage assessment revealed that masonry block production significantly affects climate change, contributing 42 % to the total damage due to carbon emissions. Human health is impacted by 30 %, mainly pollutant emissions, while resources and ecosystem quality contribute 23 % and 5.5 %, respectively. Substituting natural aggregates with recycled one decreases total damage by 6 %, reducing emissions and resource depletion. Cement remains the most significant factor in environmental damage across all categories.
