AB  - Over the years, infrastructure development has remained a key driver for projected development and employment trends in Emerging Markets and Developing Economies (EMDEs). Cement-based materials are the most consumed in infrastructure projects, yet cement contributes or is responsible for about 70% of the embodied carbon in the infrastructure industry. Production of chemical admixtures, which have been used over the time to enhance plastic and hardened-state properties of concrete, is high energy consuming and environmentally taxing which has recently attracted the use for bio-available sustainable substitutes and/or supplements such as Opuntia ficus-indica. In this chapter, experimental insights are provided on performance enhancement of Normal Strength Concrete incorporating Opuntia ficus-indica mucilage (Ofim), by mass replacement of mixing water. Plastic and hardened-state properties of Ofim and chemical admixture modified concrete mixes, prepared with varying additive proportions, are compared with those of conventional concrete mixes. Durability properties and micro-characterization of mucilage-modified mixes are also discussed herein. The Chapter is concluded with results of a comparative Environmental Life Cycle Assessment (E-LCA), conducted to assess resource use efficiency and to quantify the environmental impacts of Ofim-modified concrete relative to conventional concrete.
