AB  - Pervious concrete, also known as porous or permeable concrete, is an eco-friendly construction material acknowledged for its capacity to manage stormwater runoff, mitigate flooding, and enhance water quality. Unlike conventional concrete, it enables water infiltration into the ground and reduces surface runoff. This attribute proves invaluable in urban areas grappling with water management challenges due to the surge in impervious surfaces. The imperative for environmental sustainability in construction and infrastructure has intensified amidst climate change and resource depletion concerns. Addressing this urgency, life cycle assessment (LCA) emerges as a vital tool. LCA systematically evaluates the environmental and economic impacts of a product, process, or service from raw material extraction through production, utilization, to eventual disposal or recycling, offering a holistic perspective on sustainability in construction practices. This chapter explores the application of LCA to analyze the environmental and economic impacts of pervious concrete across its life cycle.
