AB  - A bolts-flange connected demountable and replaceable reinforced concrete column (DRCC) is developed to enhance the resilience of structures throughout the whole life cycle. When the function of the structure changes or the DRCC is damaged during its service life, the DRCC could be replaced using jacks and temporary supports. At the end of the service life, the DRCC could be dismantled and be reused after evaluation. In the design of a DRCC, the two end joints are strengthened to ensure that no damage occurs on them during load carrying. To investigate the demountable and replaceable performance of DRCCs, four specimens were designed for eccentric compression tests. Furthermore, finite element analysis was conducted with parameters of flange thickness, flange steel strength and bolt preload. Results show that the specimens all realized the desired failure mode of damage concentrated in the DRCS. When it is loaded to 0.5 Pmax, replacing the DRCS does not affect the load-bearing performance of the specimen. The replacement method proposed in this paper is easy to operate, and the process is completed with the co-operation of 1 hoisting crane and 4 workers in less than 70 min. Finally, based on the experimental study and finite element analysis, the demountable and replaceable performance is evaluated in four aspects. DRCC is considered demountable and replaceable when the conditions are simultaneously met: the load does not exceed 0.5 Pmax, the flange stress is elastic, the bolt load varies from 0.98 to 1.04, and no penetration cracks occur in DRCS.
