AB  - All Concrete contributes to approximately 2/3 of total CO2 emissions in construction, which mostly associated with the large amount of energy consumption required for processing concrete materials. Since a high-rise building has a large volume of concrete work, it will be directly proportional to the energy consumption used. The objectives of this research is to determine the energy consumption used and carbon dioxide emissions during the life cycle of concrete material for the construction of column structure by using Life Cycle Assessment (LCA). The LCA is conducted by the following steps: determining the dimensions and volume of concrete structure, calculating the energy consumption and CO2 emissions, and analysing the possible improvements to reduce the magnitude of the resulting impacts of concrete works. By using the School of Dentistry Building construction at the Brawijaya University campus in Malang as the case study, the results showed that: the total electrical energy consumption was 13.126 kWh and total fuel consumption was 12.26 litres; the total CO2 emissions produced were 44.4 kg with the highest percentage at the distribution stage (64%), while the production stage produced the lowest percentage (10%). Following the results, the study recommends that the contractors should prioritize using batching plants that are closest to the construction site, and replace diesel fuel with biodiesel for concrete material distribution. © Published under licence by IOP Publishing Ltd.
