AB  - Concrete-filled steel tubular (CFST) frame structures are widely used in earthquake regions due to their superior seismic performance, nevertheless the corresponding life cycle sustainability performance has barely been assessed. This study conducts the seismic performance analysis and sustainability evaluation on the selected 6-story and 12-story CFST frame structures, and comprehensively discusses the direct loss of considering seismic sustainability for 6-story CFST frames. The results show that the selected multi-story and high-rise CFST frame structures both have good seismic performance, meeting the performance requirement of three seismic levels in the design code. Regarding environmental impact throughout the life cycle, CFST frame structures have the greatest impact on global climate change (53 %), followed by water resource consumption (24 %). In the full life cycle cost of 6-story and 12-story CFST frame structures, the operating stage accounts for the largest proportion, namely 69.3 % and 71.43 % respectively whilst the proportions of each stage are similar. Regarding social impact, the damage of CFST frame structures to human health is more prominent in the construction stages, mainly due to the damage of dust and harmful gas emissions to the human respiratory system. In the Life cycle sustainability assessment (LCSA) of the 6-story CFST frame, Life cycle assessment (LCA) accounts for 9.7 %, Life cycle cost (LCC) accounts for 73.9 %, and Social-Life Cycle Assessment (S-LCA) accounts for 16.3 %, indicating that environmental and social impacts cannot be ignored within the lifecycle sustainability of CFST frames. The aging reduction factors have a significant impact on the prediction of sustainable losses for earthquakes throughout the life cycle, without which the maximum expected loss with the factors is only 60 % of the loss. Even though the seismic performance of high-rise buildings is slightly better than that of multi-story buildings, the direct loss of sustainability for the single story of high-rise buildings is 9–14 % higher than that of multi-story buildings, indicating that the mechanical performance and sustainability of the structure both should be considered in practical design.
