AB  - Prestressed concrete containments (PCC) are extensively used in nuclear power plants (NPP) to serve the critical function of providing an external protection and a leak proof boundary for containing radiation. These containments generally have prestressing tendons in the circumferential and meridional directions. The curvature effect of the tendons introduces radial tensile stresses in the concrete shell which are generally neglected in nuclear containment design. Thus many PCC in existing NPPs are not reinforced in radial direction. This leaves these structures vulnerable to potential laminar cracking and delamination during their life cycle. Since most of those PCCs will continue to operate for decades, their evaluation for delamination risks and safety margins are beneficial and essential to public safety. In this study, delamination risks of PCCs without radial reinforcement are evaluated based on concrete damaged plasticity (CDP) model. A nonlinear finite element (NLFE) model is developed and first validated using results from delamination tests of curved post-tensioned concrete walls. The validated NLFE model is then used to evaluate several practical examples whose parameters are based on existing PCCs of NPPs in China. Results show that calculated safety factors of delamination in example PCCs varies from 1.33 to 2.15. This finding may raise the necessity to conduct delamination risk evaluation for other existing PCCs without radial reinforcement. The presented finite element model also provides an useful tool for delamination risk assessment of existing PCCs prior to operations such as a detensioning and retensioning process.
