AB  - Ion-adsorption type rare earth tailings (RET) are a large solid waste generated during the extraction of rare earth elements from ores, urgently requiring effective disposal. From the mineralogical point of view, RET contains high content of clay minerals, which can be used as supplementary cementitious material. In this study, RET, acted as clay, in combination with limestone (LS) were used to partially replace ordinary Portland cement (OPC) in limestone calcined clay cement (LC3) production for the first time. The effects of replacement ratio on hydration kinetics, compressive strength, and microstructure of LC3 were investigated. The results showed that RET possessed high pozzolanic activity after calcination at 750°C (RET750°C), as clay minerals were transformed into amorphous phases, forming reactive silica-alumina phases. These phases reacted with portlandite produced during cement hydration to form calcium-(aluminate)-silicate-hydrate. Moreover, the synergy between RET750°C and LS promoted the formation of hemicarboaluminate and monocarboaluminate. Therefore, LC3 prepared with 20 wt% RET750°C and 10 wt% LS achieved a 28-d compressive strength comparable to OPC, while achieving considerably lower early compressive strength. To improve early-age strength, metakaolin (MK) was introduced to partially replace RET750°C. With an optimized replacement ratio of 8 wt% RET750°C by MK, the 3-, 7-, and 28-d compressive strengths of LC3 increased significantly from 5.33, 11.11, and 37.57–25.01, 40.44, and 58.32 MPa. MK enhanced the densification and homogeneity of the LC3 sample at early stage, improving early compressive strength while ensuring long-term structural stability of LC3. Life cycle assessment showed that using RET as SCM in LC3 production substantially reduced environmental burdens, achieving lower CO2 emission and energy consumption than OPC. These results highlight the advantages and potential of RET in promoting sustainable development within the construction industry. Future research on the optimization of performance of RET-derived LC3 and its application warrants to further study.
