AB  - The high volume of iron ore tailings (IOT) produced annually by mining and the difficulty of designing a sustainable destination for this material are challenges on a global scale. Currently, most of the IOT is stored in dams or piles. However, dam ruptures, geotechnical instabilities, and acid mine drainage are some evidence that we need to discuss more sustainable possibilities for the destination of IOT. The paper presents alternatives for using IOT, aligned with green and circular economy principles, as a subsidy for already consolidated production chains. In this context, the construction industry emerges as an alternative capable of absorbing part of the IOT and directing it to construction technologies. A correlation between the chemical and physical characteristics of IOT and its main applications shows the influence of the characteristics of the tailings for each application. The main challenges encountered in using IOT are the significant compositional and mineralogical heterogeneity due to differences in the ore rocks and beneficiation methods, the variation in physical-chemical properties, and legislative barriers. Part of this phenomenon lies in the challenge of understanding the various specificities of IOT and how its properties influence its application in different construction materials. Aiming to fill this knowledge gap, this literature review aims to elucidate the potential and challenges of using IOT in technologies in the construction industry. The lack of durability and life cycle assessment studies shows the demand for further research. This research was conducted using the Proknow-C Systematic Literature Review method, which selected 122 significant papers on the topic within a time frame between 2020 and 2024.
