AB  - This study addresses the interfacial bonding issue between new and old concrete in repair projects. It proposes using carbonized recycled aggregates (CRA) to enhance the interfacial bonding between the two. The macroscopic, microscopic, and nanoscale characteristics of the interface between new and old concrete were systematically investigated, and the environmental benefits of this method were discussed. Results show that the peak interfacial strain of the new/old concrete interface increased after using CRA, and the micropore content in the interfacial transition zone (ITZ) decreased by 67.2 %. Additionally, the contents of low-density and high-density C-S-H significantly increased, the ITZ thickness reduced from 70 μm to 55 μm, and the elastic modulus increased from 14.14 GPa to 17.64 GPa. The interfacial damage mode transitioned from shear to tensile with increasing interfacial roughness, exhibiting more pronounced plastic damage characteristics. Furthermore, CRA significantly improved the internal pore structure of the new/old concrete. Compared with the control group, the porosity decreased by 17.54 %, and the pore fractal dimension increased by 3.68 % after 14 days of carbonation of the CRA. The application of CRA enhanced the interfacial performance between new and old concrete. It reduced environmental impact indicators, with the global warming and photochemical ozone creation potential decreasing by 531.4 % and 27.7 %, respectively. These quantitative findings highlight the potential of CRA in improving the sustainability and performance of concrete structures.
