AB  - Utilizing thermal-activated recycled cement powder (RCP) not only addresses the issue of waste disposal and mitigates the scarcity of non-renewable resources needed for cement production, but also helps decrease the significant carbon emissions associated with the cement production process, realizes the life cycle and renewable development of cement. In this work, RCP was prepared by thermal-activated modified recycled cement as supplementary cementitious. The influence of partial substitution of cement with RCP on mortar workability, compressive strength, drying shrinkage, hydration products and microstructure were systematically investigated. In addition, the economic and environmental evaluation of mortars containing RCP was carried out. The results demonstrated that the fluidity of the mortar decreased and the setting time was shortened with RCP content increasing. The addition of a moderate amount of RCP (10 wt%) instead of cement enhances the compressive strength of the mortar, which is mainly attributed to the nucleation of fine RCP particles and the synergistic action of RCP with fly ash to generate monocarbonate. The incorporation of 10 wt% RCP increased the degree of hydration, improved the densification of the microstructure, and also reduced the long-term drying shrinkage. Additionally, mortar containing RCP shows better economic benefits and lower environmental impacts. Therefore, RCP as supplementary cementitious can be regarded as a viable and sustainable alternative for construction materials.
