AB  - This study aims to evaluate the potential of hybrid alkali-activated binders (HAAB) as sustainable and cost-effective alternatives to traditional epoxy-based adhesives for post-installation adhesive anchors in concrete. HAAB mortars were manufactured using high-calcium fly ash (FA) and hydraulic cement (HC) under varying sodium silicate solution/14 M sodium hydroxide solution (SS/SH) and river sand/binder (RS/B) ratios. Two types of anchors were investigated: threaded rods (TR) with diameters of 12 and 16 mm and round steel bars (RB) with a diameter of 12 mm. Their workability, compressive strength, drying shrinkage, pull-out performance, cost, and life-cycle performance were evaluated. The test results indicated that FA-based HAAB mortars containing HC exhibited acceptable setting times and achieved 1-d compressive strengths of up to 43.9 MPa, meeting the minimum strength requirement of 14 MPa specified in ASTM C881/C881M-20a. However, their 1-d strengths were generally lower than those of the epoxy adhesive. Moreover, the peak pull-out capacities of FA-HC-based HAAB mortars are lower than those of the epoxy adhesive, approximately 0.49–0.60 times. Although HAAB mortars had lower pull-out capacities and displacement values than epoxy adhesives, they were more cost-effective and had a smaller carbon footprint approximately 28–37 and 4.2–6.1 times, respectively. Therefore, FA-HC-based HAAB mortars, particularly those with optimized SS/SH and RS/B ratios, demonstrate significant potential as alternative adhesive materials for post-installed adhesive anchors in concrete owing to their superior cost-effectiveness and reduced carbon footprint compared to epoxy adhesives.
