AB  - To achieve a win-win solution for pollution reduction in the traditional construction industry and rubbish management, waste oyster shells (WOS) has been widely utilized as construction materials. This study systematically considered the overall potential impacts of different WOS substitutions (WOS fines, WOS sand, WOS gravel) in different cement-based products. The valorization scenarios involved applying WOS substitutions into mortar, brick, and concrete, while the basic scenario focused on the landfilling method. To highlight regional differences, Shanghai, Paris, and Seoul were selected as the primary application environments. Therefore, by considering various perspectives such as substitution types, replacement ratios, application locations, and types of construction products, this study systematically analyzed the environmental performance, mechanical strength, and economic feasibility of all scenarios. Ultimately, a comprehensive evaluation was conducted using the 3E model to identify the optimal solution. The results indicated that the environmental friendliness of WOS products was always superior to the control group, while the pollution contributions of product components can be ranked as: cement production ≈ natural aggregate transportation ≫ WOS processing and transportation. The environmental friendliness can be further improved by shortening the upstream aggregate transport distance, increasing the cement substitution ratio, and reducing electricity consumption during WOS fines production, as confirmed by AI-aided sensitivity analysis. Moreover, the analysis of economic feasibility and engineering properties demonstrated that the WOS products did not result in significant cost savings and may carry the risk of reduced strength. It can be further enhanced by lowering WOS preprocessing labor costs and incorporating a superplasticizer agent. Finally, the 3E model evaluation of all scenarios across the three regions revealed that the brick with 10 % cement replacement achieved the highest score due to its low environmental impact, high mechanical performance, and most economical production costs. This study provides new insights for decision-making regarding sustainable construction materials.
