AB  - The development of coal gangue cementitious materials as substitutes for cement provides substantial economic and environmental benefits by mitigating the environmental pollution caused by coal gangue accumulation and cement production. This study investigates the synthesis of activated coal gangue powder (ACGP) and activated coal gangue colloid (ACGC) through dry and wet grinding processes, respectively. Various mortar formulations containing ACGC and ACGP with substitution rates of 10 %, 20 %, 30 %, and 40 % were assessed using a combination of macroscopic observations, microscopic analyses, and theoretical evaluations to determine their mechanical properties, hydration characteristics, and environmental impacts. The results indicate that wet grinding for 8 hours demonstrated superior performance compared to mechanical grinding for 16 hours. ACGC exhibited higher levels of exposed SiO2 and Al2O3 than ACGP, thereby enhancing self-hydration and nucleation, leading to an improved formation of C-(A)-S-H gel. This phenomenon was correlated with increased weight loss temperatures and distinct ablation peak differences. The pore size distribution of the mortar emphasized ACGC's superior filling capability compared to ACGP. Furthermore, the morphology of hydration products in ACGC, influenced by Al/Si and Ca/(Al+Si) ratios, displayed clusters, foils, and fibrous structures. Life cycle assessment revealed that ACGP and ACGC substitution rates demonstrated an inverse correlation with environmental impacts and willingness-to-pay metrics, with ACGP showing higher values than ACGC. However, radar model analysis indicated that mortar with a 20 % ACGC substitution rate achieved optimal performance, attaining a peak geometric mean of 0.02251.
