AB  - Due to the substantial contribution of the construction industry to
greenhouse gas emissions, there exists a pressing need for the
development and adoption of sustainable alternatives. In this context,
hempcrete has emerged as a promising alternative in the pursuit of
sustainable construction practices. This paper presents a comprehensive
review of the existing research landscape surrounding hempcrete as a
sustainable building material. The review explores the primary materials
utilised in hempcrete, followed by an analysis of the opportunities and
limitations associated with the material. Furthermore, the paper
investigates diverse construction techniques and practical applications,
binders, mix designs, as well as the properties of hempcrete spanning
from fresh-state characteristics to the mechanical, durability, thermal,
and acoustic properties of the material. Moreover, the environmental
sustainability as well as the cost of hempcrete construction are
investigated. Upon analysis of the research presented in this review
paper, it becomes apparent that hempcrete demonstrates noteworthy
potential as a sustainable solution in the construction sector,
emphasising its role in reducing the environmental impacts brought about
by the construction industry. This review paper presents the findings of
the current research landscape of hempcrete and provides insights for
future research endeavours aimed at optimising the performance of
hempcrete.
