AB  - There is a need to find alternatives to the extraction of non-renewable
mineral resources for the production of new binders. Some specific
building materials such as plant-aggregate-based concretes do not
require high-strength binders. In this context, the lime-pozzolan
technology, inherited from Roman times, can help reduce the carbon
footprint of finished materials by the use of low amounts of lime. In
this paper, three different marine dredged sediments were used as
natural alumino-silicate resources and blended with hydrated lime. Based
on previous literature dealing with the pozzolanic activity of
cristallized minerals, the sediments were reduced to micronized powders.
The hydration of these systems was investigated through hardening and
reaction kinetics up to 365 days at 20 degrees C and 50 degrees C. The
sediments used revealed two kinds of mineral assemblages and the best
pozzolanic reactivity was found with quartz-rich sediments. In addition,
the optimum lime content was found to be 20 wt% until 180 days.(c) 2023
The Society of Powder Technology Japan. Published by Elsevier B.V. and
The Society of Powder Technology Japan. All rights reserved.
