AB  - For soft soils, creep settlement plays an important role in the full
life cycle performance of infrastructures, which is a serious concern
for engineers and researchers. Columns such as deep cement mixed (DCM)
soil columns are commonly adopted to treat soft grounds in order to
reduce the life cycle settlement of the infrastructures. However, the
creep behavior of soft grounds treated by DCM soil columns is often
ignored, inducing underestimated total settlements or unexpected
differential settlements. In this study, a new calculation method is
developed for the life cycle settlement of column-improved soft grounds
by considering the creep of soft soils and load transfer between the
columns and the surrounding soils. A physical model test with
double-layer soil improved by DCM soil columns is designed and performed
to demonstrate the feasibility of the calculation method. It is found
that the settlements calculated by the proposed method show good
agreement with the measured data in the physical model. A parametric
study is conducted, revealing that the calculated settlement of the
double-layer soil improved by DCM columns can be largely influenced by
the stress concentration ratio, the permeability of the DCM columns, and
the area replacement ratio. Finally, the proposed method is applied to
calculate the settlement in a real project.
