AB  - This article presents a tool to predict the economic and environmental
impacts of the life cycle of residential buildings in the design phase
based on an architectural project; the project's budget; construction
costs databases, in particular those of Andalucia, Spain; and the
ecological footprint indicator. The tool proposes alternatives in
resource use (materials, manpower and machinery) and construction
systems, as these can be used in decision making to improve the impact
of the building's life cycle. The case of a specific ten-floor
residential building is analyzed, including the resources used and their
general and specific economic and environmental impacts according to the
stages of the project. The materials were found to be the resource of
greatest importance, with the concrete or ceramic in particular
producing the greatest impact. A sensitivity analysis was carried out in
which different material alternatives were proposed for a building
solution, and data was obtained to decide on the most economically and
environmentally viable option. The tool is easy to use and can be the
basis for building certification and the development of standard values
for use in government policies.
