AB  - The assessment of asphalt pavement structures with diversified designs
and constructions has been a critical challenge due to the practical
limitations in testing and methodology. Here, we propose a complex
network approach to the evaluation of longitudinal asphalt pavement
performances in the Research Institute of Highway Ministry of Transport
track (RIOHTrack). We add to the growing field in which network
approaches are employed and indicate the profound application in
understanding pavement structures. Specifically, similar and disparate
pavement performances are revealed regardless of their predefined design
and construction categories. Evidence shows that short-term pavement
performance depends on the strength of the subbase structure, whereas
long-term performance relies on the thickness of the asphalt layer. In
addition, our results indicate that recycling materials could be an
important substitute for reducing the thickness of asphalt concrete and
maintaining a healthy service life.
