AB  - This review article aims to suggest recycling technological options in
India and illustrates plastic recycling clusters and reprocessing
infrastructure for plastic waste (PW) recycling in India. The study
shows that a majority of states in India are engaged in recycling, road
construction, and co-processing in cement kilns while reprocessing
capabilities among the reprocessors are highest for polypropylene (PP)
and polyethylene (PE) polymer materials. This review suggests that there
are key opportunities for mechanical recycling, chemical recycling,
waste-to-energy approaches, and bio-based polymers as an alternative to
deliver impact to India's PW problem. On the other hand, overall,
polyurethane, nylon, and polyethylene terephthalate appear most
competitive for chemical recycling. Compared to conventional fossil fuel
energy sources, polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), and polystyrene
are the three main polymers with higher calorific values suitable for
energy production. Also, multi-sensor-based artificial intelligence and
blockchain technology and digitization for PW recycling can prove to be
the future for India in the waste flow chain and its management.
Overall, for a circular plastic economy in India, there is a necessity
for a technology-enabled accountable quality-assured collaborative
supply chain of virgin and recycled material.
