AB  - This study is a life-cycle analysis of the balance of system (BOS)
components of the 3.5 MWP multi-crystalline PV installation at Tucson
Electric Power's (TEP) Springerville, AZ field PV plant. TEP instituted
all innovative PV installation program guided by design optimization and
cost minimization. The advanced design of the PV structure incorporated
the weight of the PV modules as all element of support design, thereby
eliminating the need for concrete foundations. The estimate of the
life-cycle energy requirements embodied in the BOS is 542 MJ/m(2), a
71% reduction front those of all older central plant; the corresponding
life-cycle greenhouse gas emissions are 29 kg CO2 eq./m(2). From field
measurements, the energy payback time (EPT) of the BOS is 0.21 years for
the actual location of this plant, and 0.37 years for average US
insolation/temperature conditions. This is a great improvement from the
EPT of about 1.3 years, estimated for all older central plant. The total
cost of the balance of system components was $940 US per kW(P) of
installed PV, all other milestone in improvement. These results were
verified with data from different databases and further tested with
sensitivity- and data-uncertainty analyses. Copyright (c) 2005 John
Wiley & Sons, Ltd.
