AB  - End of Life Tires (ELT) constitute a major portion of End of life
Vehicles (ELV). The treatment process of ELTs is primarily aimed at
recovering steel and rubber, which jointly represent the main portion of
the ELT material and are currently applied in different sectors. During
the treatment of ELTs, other sub-products are generated in significant
quantities (about 10-15% in weight), as textile fibers that currently
are landfilled or used for energy recovery. The aim of this study is a
comparative evaluation of the environmental impacts related to three
different end of life scenarios for the textile fibers. In addition to
landfilling and incineration, this study considers the possibility to
reuse textile fibers as reinforcement in bituminous conglomerates.
Results obtained through the Life Cycle Assessment study confirms that
the reuse scenario leads to a relevant reduction of impacts in terms of
Global Warming Potential. However, by considering other environmental
metrics the reuse scenario is not always the less impactful one. (C)
2018 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V.
