AB  - In this piece, two very concrete aspects of the theories that deal with
territorial agglomerations of companies (or clusters) are analyzed.
These are, on the one hand, the factors that cause the development of
such clusters, studying their origins and life cycles in a particular
way. On the other hand, we look at those policies and actions that can
be carried out in order to harness these territorial agglomerations.
The work aims to obtain a holistic vision of the aforementioned aspects,
drawing from significant studies from both academia and the private
sector.
Among the main conclusions of the article, we highlight the fact that
clusters always have a stressed life cycle, in which both internal and
external factors influence the formation of clusters. In this sense, the
factors that cause a cluster to remain in its stages of growth and
maturity for as long as possible, without entering into a phase of
decay, are indicated.
