AB  - The structural performance of steel braced frames equipped with a new
typology of dissipative replaceable components (called DRBrC) is
analyzed and compared to the one of a traditional Concentrically Braced
Frame (CBF) with diagonal-tension-only braces, highlighting pros and
cons of the different solutions. The main benefit of the proposed system
lies in the easy replacement of the damaged dissipative components after
the earthquake, allowing to speed up retrofit operations without
stopping the activities inside the building. The results of low-cycle
assessment and life cycle cost analysis performed in the framework of
the European research project DISSIPABLE confirmed the economic and
environmental convenience of the system for repairing operations after
seismic events. In the present work, two case study buildings, owing the
same geometry and designed for the same loads with and without DRBrC
devices, are analyzed and compared in terms of overall seismic
performance, relevant parameters versus Intensity Measure (IM)
indicators and fragility curves. An in-depth dissertation about design
criteria, numerical modelling techniques and analysis procedures in the
nonlinear field to be used for the DRBrC frames is provided.
