AB  - Urbanization and demographic growth have led to increased global energy
consumption in recent years. Furthermore, construction products and
materials industries have contributed significantly to this increase in
fossil fuel use, due to their significant energy requirements, and
consequent environmental impact, during the extraction and processing of
raw materials. To address this environmental problem, architectural
design and civil engineering are trying to implement strategies that
enable the use of high-performance materials while minimizing the usage
of energy-intensive or toxic and dangerous building materials. These
efforts also aim to make buildings less energy-consuming during their
useful life. Using waste materials, such as Construction and Demolition
Waste (CdW), is one of the most promising approaches to address this
issue. In recent years, the European Union (EU) has supported recovery
strategies focused on using CdW, as they account for more than 30% of
the total waste production in the EU. In this regard, reuse
techniques-such as incorporating concrete fragments and bricks as road
floor fillers-have been the subject of targeted scientific research.
This review will outline various strategies for producing green cement
and concrete, particularly emphasizing the reuse of Construction and
Demolition Waste (CdW).
