AB  - This study evaluates the environmental impacts of geopolymer concrete
mixtures activated by alkali activators compared to traditional CEM-I
concrete mixtures using the life cycle assessment method. The study
employs the Environmental Product Declarations framework to evaluate the
raw material supply chain, ensuring data comparability. Environmental
Cost Indicator (ECI) is used to determine relevant environmental impact
categories. New concrete mixtures are developed to increase technical
performance and identify suitable applications for geopolymer concrete.
The compressive strength of developed geopolymer concrete mixtures after
material composition analysis is evaluated and compared to traditional
CEM-I concrete mixtures. The social supply chain due diligence of the
concrete mixtures is also investigated and evaluated within the context
of existing or upcoming regulations for the construction sector.
Results: show that all geopolymer concrete mixtures indicate lower
environmental impacts, particularly in terms of Global Warming Potential
and ECI. The reduction in ECI for different geopolymer concrete mixtures
ranges from less than 20 % to above 60 % compared to studied CEM-I
recipes. Despite the lower strength of the developed geopolymer concrete
mixtures in comparison to CEM-I concrete mixtures, their performance was
sufficient to meet the strength class for precast non-structural
elements. The social supply chain due diligence analysis indicated
potential social risks along the supply chain that may increase with the
use of alternative noncementitious binders or other additives. The study
suggests a source-specific analysis of raw materials integrated with
EPDs to expand them into Environmental-Social-Performance-Product-
Declarations. This study provides insights into the environmental and
social impacts of geopolymer concrete mixtures.
