AB  - The main structural elements of Reinforced Concrete (RC) bridges
deteriorate with time under uncertain hazards such as scouring and
corrosive conditions. In this paper, a multi-hazard framework is
proposed to assess the performance of bridges subjected to the main
effective uncertain natural hazards during their lifetime. In this
assessment, the uncertainties associated with the combined effects of
multiple hazards including pier scour, earthquake, carbonation, and
corrosion are considered. Therefore, for each, hazard their severity
relationships have been extracted over time, and the effects of these
hazards on concrete elements are presented as resistance dimming
parameters. The annual and cumulative losses due to these hazards in
unit of currency are estimated using the life-cycle cost analysis. For
decision making, the value of the expected cumulative cost associated
with the retrofit or design scenarios is evaluated using a cost-benefit
analysis. The results for a case study bridge, obtained from the
framework proposed in the present study, indicated the significant
impact of the different scenarios on reducing the expected value of
damages or incurred losses due to multiple hazards. (C) 2021 Sharif
University of Technology. All rights reserved.
