AB  - Concrete is one of the most widely used construction materials in the
world. However, the production of Portland cement as the essential
constituent of concrete requires a considerable energy level. Also
releases a significant amount of chemical carbon dioxide emissions and
other greenhouse gases (GHGs) into the atmosphere. Global demand will
increase almost 200 % by 2050 from 2010 levels. Thus, seeking an
eco-efficient and sustainable concrete may be one of the main roles that
the construction industry should play in sustainable construction.
Portland cement can be partially replaced by cementitious and pozzolanic
materials, especially those of industry by-products such as fly ash,
GOBS, silica fume, ceramic waste powder and metamorphic rock dust from
stone cutting industry. The aggregates are also conserved by replacing
them with recycled or waste materials (among which recycled concrete),
ceramic waste, post-consumer glass, and recycled tires. All of the
previous alternatives are, currently, the most used. This paper
summarizes current knowledge about eco-efficient concrete, by reviewing
previously published work.
