AB  - This study presents a sustainable method for recycling Ordinary Portland
cement concrete (OPCC) using geopolymer technology, reducing the need
for new cement and aggregates. By including geopolymerization, the
process offers significant ecological and economic advantages over
conventional methods. Crushed and pulverized waste concrete was used as
recycled granulate (RA) and binder, using recycled clay brick powder
(RBP) as an additive. Based on the results, the incorporation of RA into
recycled geopolymer concrete (GRC) improved the compressive strength by
11.7%, with a maximum compressive strength of 44.3 MPa and a tensile
strength of 4.77 MPa. Replacing recycled concrete powder (RCP) with 5%
wt% resulted in a 21% reduction in compressive strength (35 MPa) and a
25.3% reduction in tensile strength (3.56 MPa). A further increase in
the RBP content to 15 wt% reduced the compressive strength to 27.65 MPa
(37.5% reduction). The RBP content directly effects on the flowability
and improves the appearance of the GRC and reduces cracks. When
comparing total eco-cost, GRC (128.9 <euro>/m3) proved to be more
environmentally friendly than OPCC (140.4 <euro>/m3). The developed GRC
meets construction standards and offers a promising alternative to
conventional recycling and exceeds OPCC in terms of eco-cost and
sustainability.
