AB  - The results of life-cycle assessments (LCAs) of concrete are highly
dependent on the concrete design method. In this study, LCAs were
conducted to evaluate the environmental impacts of the replacement of
sand with furnace bottom-ash (FBA) in concrete. In the FBA-based
concretes, sand was replaced with FBA at proportions of 0, 30, 50, 70,
and 100 wt%. Two design methods were studied: (i) concrete with fixed
slump ranges of 0-10 mm (CON-fix-SLUMP-0-10) and 30-60 mm
(CON-fix-SLUMP-30-60); and (ii) concrete with fixed water/cement (W/C)
ratios of 0.45 (CON-fix-W/C-0.45) and 0.55 (CON-fix-W/C-0.55). The
ReCiPe2016 midpoint and single-score (six methodological options)
methods were used to compare the environmental damage caused by the
FBA-based concretes. A two-stage nested (hierarchical) analysis of
variance (ANOVA) was used to simultaneously evaluate the results of six
ReCiPe2016 methodologies. The ReCiPe2016 results indicate that replacing
sand with FBA decreased the environmental impact of the concretes with
fixed slump ranges and increased the environmental impact of the
concretes with fixed W/C ratios. Therefore, using FBA as a partial sand
replacement in concrete production is of debatable utility, as its
impact highly depends on the concrete design method used.
