AB  - The use of stainless steel in high corrosion risk areas represents a
viable option for reducing the life-cycle cost and extending the service
life of concrete structures. However, the possible galvanic corrosion
between it and carbon steel continues to be a concern. In this article,
the galvanic coupling behaviours of carbon steel and three different
stainless steels were investigated in simulated pore solutions and
concrete specimens. The results showed that the oxygen reduction
reaction was much lower on stainless steel than on passive carbon steel,
leading to a lower galvanic coupling current between stainless steel and
corroding carbon steel than that between passive and corroding carbon
steels. However, rust contamination of stainless steel was found to
increase galvanic coupling corrosion on carbon steel.
