AB  - Blending is an activity aimed at treating and preparing waste in the
form of blends that are intended for co-processing in cement industries.
The objective of this study was to identify the impacts generated in the
different blending stages. Through Life Cycle Assessment, impacts were
estimated on an average production profile of 30.650 t/year of blends.
The system boundary approach was gate to gate and the functional unit 1
t of blends. The impact assessment methodologies used were ReCiPe and
Impact 2002+. The results show that, by the ReCiPe method, Human
Toxicity was responsible for 53% of the total impact, caused by the
emission of metals such as selenium, manganese, arsenic and barium.
Using the Impact 2002+ method, Inorganic Inhalables had the highest
total impact (51.8%), mainly caused by nitrogen oxides. It is concluded
that blending has a greater impact on human health, especially on the
health of workers, who suffer from greater exposure. It is recommended
that the activity be reassessed by the environmental and labor control
bodies, prioritizing process automation studies, epidemiological
studies, and continuing the investigation of the impacts of blends
burning in clinker kilns in a cradle to gate'' approach.
