AB  - Greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions are the primary cause of the growing
worry over global warming. The manufacturing of cement is a significant
source of carbon dioxide emissions into the environment, which is
driving up demand for sustainable building practices. Historically,
there has been a lack of fully waste-based green cement, with most
cementitious materials incorporating only a partial proportion of waste
materials. However, recent advancements have led to the development of a
supplementary cementitious material (SCM) that is entirely derived from
waste byproducts. While numerous geopolymers and alkaline-activated
cements (AAC) have been explored in previous research, these typically
rely on commercial activators to activate the binder. In contrast, the
innovative binder under investigation in this study is activated
exclusively using alkaline compounds derived from waste categories,
presenting a unique and sustainable approach to cement production. This
advancement marks a significant step towards the creation of an entirely
waste-based, eco-friendly cement solution. After 56 days, the
waste-based green cement binder had a compressive strength of 22 MPa.
This WGC is ideal for general construction applications. Subsequently,
the developed Waste-Based Green Cement (WGC) was tested by incorporating
varying proportions of Ordinary Portland Cement (OPC) to evaluate the
optimal replacement level of OPC with WGC. This investigation aimed to
determine the most effective substitution ratio, ensuring that the
resulting composite material maintained the required performance
characteristics while maximizing the use of waste-derived components.
There is a possibility of a 50 % cut-off of ordinary Portland cement
(OPC) with WGC, resulting 42 MPa after 28 days of ambient curing
condition (maintained temperature 21 +/- 2 degrees C and maintained
relative humidity 50 +/- 10 %). Mineralogical and microstructural
variations influence on the mechanical performance of this WGC-OPC
composites and here in this paper, it highlights. Furthermore, for the
waste-based green cement mix, the optimized initial molar ratios of
SiO2/Al2O3, Na2O/Al2O3, and H2O/Na2O were obtained as 3, 1, and 9,
respectively. WGC has a unique ability to be reused after demolition
because recast samples demonstrated a significant compressive strength
of 6 MPa after 28 days of ambient curing. To address the long-term
demands of ultra-high strength applications, this research aims to
investigate the development of a novel sustainable Waste-Based Green
Cement (WGC) while simultaneously enhancing its properties.
